<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aghlabid dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/800">800</a>–909</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Aghlabids</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Aghlabid dynasty ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifriqiya">Ifriqiya</a>, roughly present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a> and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kairouan">Kairouan</a> as hereditary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> under nominal Abbasid authority from 800 to 909. In 827 they began the long conquest of Byzantine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, which created the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of Sicily. They built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-mosque-of-kairouan">Great Mosque of Kairouan</a> in its present form and were overthrown by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a> in 909.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aghlabid-dynasty.jpg" alt="Aghlabid dynasty" width="320" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Nanoxyde at French Wikipedia. · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aghlabids_Dynasty_800_-_909_(AD).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Aghlabids were an Arab dynasty from the tribe of Banu Tamim. In 800 the Abbasid caliph <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harun-al-rashid">Harun al-Rashid</a> appointed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-i-ibn-al-aghlab">Ibrahim I ibn al-Aghlab</a>, son of a Khurasani Arab commander, as hereditary emir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifriqiya">Ifriqiya</a>, in response to the anarchy that had followed the fall of the Muhallabids. There were perhaps 100,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> in the province, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berbers</a> still formed the great majority. Though independent in all but name, the dynasty never ceased to recognise Abbasid overlordship, paying an annual tribute of 800,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dirham">dirhams</a> and naming the caliph in the Friday sermon. Ibrahim built a new capital, al-Abbasiyya, outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kairouan">Kairouan</a> between 801 and 810, partly to escape the Malikite jurists who condemned the luxury of the dynasty and its Mu'tazilite theology.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The army combined Arab troops, the jund, with slaves recruited to counterbalance them. The jund rose in revolt in 824 under Mansur ibn Nasr al-Tunbudhi, occupying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kairouan">Kairouan</a>, and the emir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziyadat-allah-i">Ziyadat Allah I</a> crushed the rising in 827. In that year the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> began, under the qadi Asad ibn al-Furat, after the Byzantine rebel Euphemios asked for help. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Palermo</a> fell in 831 and became the capital of Muslim Sicily, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/messina">Messina</a> in 842 or 843, Syracuse in 878 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taormina">Taormina</a> in 902. The Aghlabids also raided the Italian mainland, held Taranto from 840 to 880, and conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malta">Malta</a> in 870.</p><p>In 876 Ibrahim II ibn Ahmad moved to a new palace-city, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raqqada">Raqqada</a>. The dynasty declined under him: a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> revolt was put down with much loss of life, and from 893 the Isma'ili Fatimid movement grew among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kutama-berbers">Kutama Berbers</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-abdallah-al-shii">Abu Abdallah al-Shi'i</a>. Ibrahim II died besieging <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cosenza">Cosenza</a> in October 902. After the Aghlabid army was routed near al-Aribus on 18 March 909, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziyadat-allah-iii">Ziyadat Allah III</a> fled towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimid Caliphate</a> was established that year.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The Aghlabids built many of the oldest Islamic-era monuments in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a>, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ribat">Ribat</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sousse">Sousse</a>, the Ribat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bitola">Monastir</a>, the Great Mosque of Sousse and the Aghlabid Reservoirs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kairouan">Kairouan</a>. Ziyadat Allah I completely rebuilt the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-mosque-of-kairouan">Great Mosque of Kairouan</a> in 836. Its minaret is the oldest surviving one in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>, and its minbar, of carved teakwood, is the oldest surviving in the world.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Aghlabid dynasty” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He did so partly to distance himself from the Malikite jurists and theologians, who condemned the luxurious life of the <b>Aghlabids</b>, disliked their Mu'tazilite theology and Hanafi law, and objected to the unequal treatment of the Muslim Berbers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aghlabid-emirate">Aghlabid emirate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 909 his Kutama army overthrew the <b>Aghlabid</b> emirate, and the imam was retrieved from Sijilmasa and proclaimed caliph at Raqqada in January 910 as Abdallah al-Mahdi Billah, establishing a rival caliphate to Baghdad.</blockquote><a href="/empire/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimid Caliphate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Idris II broke that dependence by welcoming Arab settlers at Walili and appointing Arabs as his vizier and qadi; when the Awraba leader Ishak plotted against his life with the <b>Aghlabids</b> of Tunisia, Idris had his former protector killed and in 809 left Walili for Fez.</blockquote><a href="/empire/idrisid-dynasty">Idrisid dynasty · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After a siege, Melite, the capital of Malta, the last island around Sicily in Byzantine hands, fell to the <b>Aghlabids</b>, reinforced by a fleet sent from Sicily.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-29">29 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Aghlabids</b> had begun their conquest of Byzantine Sicily in 827.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-21">21 May in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In 827 the <b>Aghlabids of Ifriqiya</b> invaded Sicily at the invitation of the rebel Byzantine commander Euphemius.</blockquote><a href="/state/emirate-of-sicily">Emirate of Sicily</a></li></ul></section>
